# Development Workbench and presentation

Project principles are author-owned files in the selected worktree. Generate them from the Spec Kit constitution template when missing or unfinished; reuse existing principles for ordinary tasks. Revise principles only for an explicit principle change and keep task-specific scope and checks in feature documents. Read the current local principles in later stages. Ordinary development does not require a separate adoption or task version choice. Retain Birdview and project supplement source identities, applicability, and gaps. Execution preview freezes the actual local inputs through the existing execution contract.

The saved update retains the actual title, summary, artifact versions, classified pending items, and blockers. Display known information even when the complete workflow is unavailable. Only document blockers become current blockers. Unknown or unverified items retain their original status. The card is fixed to its saved receipt. The user can return to it after opening the current Workbench or reading a document. Preserve the original status, content, and conclusions of each historical card.

Use the Workbench to read artifacts, versions, sources, check results, and actual state. Use native question dialogs in the current Codex session to collect clarification, stage confirmation, and revision feedback. The Workbench has no stage confirmation button, revision feedback form, or submission entry point. The home view centers on the actual current stage and prioritizes real blockers, artifacts, and recent substantive progress. Record document self-checks, stage confirmation, validation item selection, and actual execution separately.

## Display and ongoing records

Use full-row rectangular interactive surfaces for separated artifact, acceptance, check, and reference navigation rows. The hover background fills the row header height. Reserve rounded themed controls for standalone tool buttons. An artifact row shows its title, adjacent version or “Choose a version,” and a source arrow at the right. Open named drafts and historical originals from the existing version list. Avoid guessing the default version or adding a separate draft button or status paragraph. Reading and expansion remain distinct actions and keyboard focus targets. Static rows give no indication that they can be clicked. Expanded version rows keep the same full-row rectangle with consistent hover, press, and keyboard focus feedback. They have no blue selection background. Stage, all-artifact, and original-document version lists omit “Open stage record by default” and duplicate descriptions of purpose. Stage headings, tasks, preview, and reference pages avoid repeating their purpose or mechanics. Stage summaries retain their themed emphasis, padding, and corners. Keep artifact titles and action arrows on the same row at narrow widths, wrapping long titles inside the content column. Preserve actual versions, pending items, gaps, sources, and confirmation and execution states.

In Execution Preview, keep the blue stage summary, then two consecutive rectangular entries for the change impact map and acceptance plan, followed by compact stage artifacts. Open the map in a dedicated reader within the same Workbench to free space used by stage navigation and artifacts. Keep the back control and visible plan identity, without adding a map toolbar. The original static architecture view omits activity replay. Preserve the full planned activities, sources, and unmapped scope in the exact receipt. The static map records a prediction. The acceptance plan lists all functional acceptance titles and real statuses. Expand each item directly to its conditions and evidence methods in a single level. Implementation shows incomplete tasks and the actual completed and total counts by default. The all-tasks page collapses completed tasks in one level. Index all artifacts by their actual stages and keep materials without a stage association separate as a gap. Back navigation restores scroll, focus, and expanded items. Data updates preserve the reading position.

The planned impact map shows the modules and relationships affected by this change. Use pinned planning inputs and local impact analysis with the original Birdview renderer. Use the published Server architecture only as a reference, independent of the prerequisites for this impact map. Track unmapped files, predictions, and actual acceptance separately. Exclude stale maps and maps from other runs. Show change scope, check methods, and repair limits directly when reading the exact Execution Preview artifact. Skip additional summary links, separate disclosures, and inclusion in acceptance or artifact counts. Preserve the historical scope when reading historical versions.

Artifact entries show the fixed deliverables for each stage, including principles, specification, plan, task list, and consistency analysis. Preview, implementation, and acceptance use their existing impact view, code, and actual check results, respectively. Exclude self-check preparation, command responses, process JSON, review drafts, and internal receipts from artifact and document counts. Keep using the original internal records for source and version reads.

Start the local Web workbench through the Kit entry described below and open its ready URL in the right browser panel. Stage navigation starts in the collapsed numbered sidebar. Open documents, check results, and project references in named views. Returning restores the reading context. Browsing other stages only shows explicitly associated artifacts and records. Keep the actual current stage and distinguish global references from stage data. Reading and browsing leave the workflow state unchanged. An explicitly selected MCP view can still use `formsy workbench serve PROJECT --workspace-root EXPLICIT_ROOT`; the default local view has no MCP requirement.

Before each visible development reply, save only that reply's actual changes with the existing CLI `workbench publish PROJECT --input EVENT.json`, using the exact project runtime prefix. The local page picks up the saved record through polling. No MCP display call is required. Record answers before starting a long document. Reuse question IDs, retain the actual answer source, and leave unanswered questions open. Present the first known questions for each module together while collecting each answer separately. Ask any new questions when they arise. Keep the final documents, evidence, and open items in their original versioned records.

When creating or revising a stage document, name it for the requirement and the substantive change. “Revision 5” identifies the fifth revision of the same document and needs its own review description. Register the exact `path/revision/sha256/summary` under `document_changes` and record each addition, modification, and removal under `content_changes`. Set `kind` to `added/modified/removed`, `section` to a locatable heading or requirement ID, `content` to the concrete content, and `reason` to the reason for the change. For additions and modifications, quote `content` verbatim from the registered version. A removal entry is the agent's removal note. Do not infer the original text from the current file. When the full previous original must be retained and the total event is at most 128 KiB, explicitly attach its UTF-8 `content` matching the same digest. Report unavailable historical versions as gaps. Read the full document through the same Workbench's exact source reader. Avoid substitute HTML and content without an identified source.

Use empty `document_changes` for questions, feedback, and replies with no document changes. Reusing an unchanged summary does not create a revision. Record user decisions, agent proposals, and open questions under their existing `decisions/questions/pending` contract and use the existing check entry point for document checks. Keep rendering observations separate from publication and execution. Give stage reviewers the concrete artifact version and its original reader or file reference. Receipts, reading, refresh, switching documents, errors, and recovery leave stage confirmation untouched. For missing content, a failed read, or a stale document, identify the gap and offer retry. Claim reading or review only with actual evidence.

Supply `workflow_ref:{run_id,revision,stage}` in development interactions using the actual latest flow context. Keep `interaction_id` and `reply_id` unique per project. A retry of the same event returns its original receipt. Use new IDs for new content. A run shows its own questions and answers. Other work items' histories remain separate. A reply event does not imply a native task or Milestone.

For a separately tracked review item, link `pending[].review_question_id` to `questions[].id` in the same event when asking. On receiving an actual answer, record it against the matching flow, stage, revision, and session before advancing. When the entire pending item is resolved, add its ID and resolution reason to `resolves` in the same event. Split items covering other risks or retain the unresolved portion. If an answer is recorded while the item remains open, the Workbench reminds the user to check it. Confirmation follows the existing workflow gate, and the item requires explicit resolution.

The local Web workbench is the default development view. It does not require an MCP connection or a render ACK. Static `render-flow` and `flow render` exports retain their original compatibility restrictions. The explicit `flow serve --workbench` and managed `flow web` use the same current view and saved-artifact readers. MCP cards remain optional historical/diagnostic views. Keep historical HTML and real delivery failures.

## Document self-check and user questions

The current Codex session performs document self-checks at document stages. Acceptance documents in `finish/reverify` are outside document self-check, and update publication, display, stage submission, and confirmation do not require a document self-check receipt at those stages. The Workbench displays the original check items and actual results in a read-only list. The detail page shows findings, next steps, and cited evidence. Keep approval controls, bulk approval, and recheck forms out of this read-only view.

When the user needs to make a choice, supply information, or settle conflicting feedback, prefer the native Codex `request_user_input_async` dialog and collect answers separately. If it is unavailable, ask numbered questions in the current chat. Preserve the original question IDs and actual answer sources, then update the Workbench after recording answers. Continue checking unreviewed items, insufficient evidence, and implementation checks in Codex. Ask for user input only when it is needed to resolve a question.

Opening a dialog, leaving its default selected, and timing out provide no answer or approval. A user statement does not supply missing evidence, rewrite the original check result, or automatically confirm a document or stage. Record genuine, explicit, in-scope input. Wait for a real answer before recording a satisfaction statement or advancing. Refresh and browsing do not trigger dialogs.

At `constitution`, `specify/clarify`, and `plan`, review the current draft and adopted upstream documents as each draft is created. At `constitution` review the principle draft, at `specify/clarify` include the specification, and at `plan` include the technical draft. The `tasks_analyze` and `preview` document gates cover principles, specification, technical draft, tasks, and consistency analysis. Check current versions before preview. At `finish/reverify` continue from existing requirement acceptance, findings, repair tasks, current program checks, and the original Verifiers for adopted knowledge. Revisit pending, failed, unknown, or unsupported results and the actual effects of changes. First-time requirements without current evidence remain unverified. Rerun verification for changed acceptance assertions or targets. Keep unaffected valid results, prior failures, and document self-check history. Report the actual status of any explicit independent audit. A zero exit code does not establish requirement or Verifier acceptance, and user statements do not supply test evidence.

Record and display general SOP methods separately from project knowledge supplements. The general profile checks local documents and can complete the built-in workflow without Server authorization. The server profile reads through the authorization described in [Server project data](server-project-data.md). The machine catalog covers a declared subset. Track full PC/TC/KB/TASK, security, quality, and domain applicability review separately from L1/L2/L3 gaps. A subset receipt only establishes coverage of that subset. Treat missing Server content as a supplemental gap and continue general self-check. Leave the relevant check incomplete if current atomic items have not been compared, evidence is invalid, or a document blocker remains. Failed or unverified applicable Verifiers still block acceptance. Display synchronization status separately. Preserve versions and original records for UI state changes and stage operations. See [the three project supplements](optional-project-context.md).

## Stage confirmation and revision feedback

After a guided flow actually enters `awaiting_review`, show the artifacts and check results and collect the current decision with the native `request_user_input_async` dialog. Identify the task, stage, and actual artifact version. Explain that confirmation follows the existing continuation to the next stage and that final-stage confirmation ends the workflow. Offer explicit choices to confirm, request changes, or defer, plus free text. If the native tool is unavailable, ask in the current chat and state the limitation. Use no substitute web form. Honor a user's read-only or no-advance restriction by leaving stage decisions and interactions for the restricted task untouched.

When opening the dialog, record the actual `project/run/stage/revision/input_digests` and the displayed version as question scope. Use the question ID, choice, and comments returned by the real host. Opening the dialog, default choices, timeout, reading, and refresh do not constitute an answer or approval. Keep the stage awaiting review until an answer arrives. Refresh never triggers a new dialog. Avoid repeating an unanswered question for the same version.

When the actual answer arrives, bind it to the original question ID, actual answer source, and scope. Use a stable `operation_id` and original inputs to build a `flow interact PROJECT --request FILE` request. Use `confirm` only for explicit confirmation of that stage. Preserve the user's actual feedback with `revise`. If the user requests changes without explaining them, ask for the missing feedback using the native dialog. Do not call `interact` after a deferral, dismissal, or unanswered question. Before submitting, verify the same project, run, stage, and displayed version. Reject stale answers using existing validation and obtain review for the new artifact. The CLI's status, evidence, version, and idempotency checks still apply. Update Workbench state after publishing the actual outcome.

An accepted `confirm` only moves the engine into the new current stage. The continuation binds the actual run, source confirmation operation, and next stage. Read the latest context and execute that stage automatically using its latest `revision/input_digests`. At `awaiting_review`, use the native dialog to wait for the next actual decision. Keep guided mode. A repeated answer does not repeat approval or work. Stop at final-stage confirmation. Keep adoption, publication, and upload under their separate authorization. Confirmation does not grant additional execution, deployment, or upload authority.

## Other Workbench actions and legacy requests

For other Workbench actions, `formsy_workbench_flow_prepare_action` saves the exact run, revision, input digests, action, and operation ID in that run's requests directory. Preserve the original feedback in saved requests. The conversation handoff contains a short English action and a bound request link whose target carries the SHA-256 of the original bytes and the action. Pass the action shown in the handoff explicitly with `--request-action`. For saved confirm or revise requests and existing rerun or guided actions, use `flow interact PROJECT --request-ref REF --request-action ACTION`. For other actions, first validate and read the saved request through `flow context PROJECT --request-ref REF --request-action ACTION --json`, then use the existing action interface.

Knowledge reads can increment the revision. Use the latest revision and input digests from the current context for stage execution, preserving the original run, action, and request bytes. Keep continue and confirm distinct. Reject missing digests, altered bytes, wrong actions, unsafe paths, mismatched projects, runs or operations, stale inputs, and conflicting requests under the same ID. Refresh an old card that lacks a digest. Approval still requires its original evidence. Keep raw JSON, digests, and internal execution instructions out of the visible reply.

The current Workbench prepares requests only for existing separate actions, such as execution, reads, selection, and upload. Use the established stage flow for `confirm/revise`. Keep saved legacy stage requests subject to the digest, action, and version rules above. Preserve history and its original approval status. `continue` only executes the current stage, and `refresh` only reads. A request awaiting manual submission is shown as pending submission. After asking to return it to the conversation, show it as awaiting handling. Show it as handled only after actual receipt and CLI acceptance. When a host is busy and queues a request, avoid repeat clicks. Saving validation items is separate from executing checks. A native card requires the host's actual `callTool` save channel to prepare a background request. Report a missing channel. Do not construct browser-side JSON or fabricate delivery.

On stage entry or resume, publish `in_progress` with a concrete summary and the actual current run, revision, and stage. Publish the actual outcome when work finishes or is interrupted, without waiting for stage submission. The backend binds the current attempt and basis. Callers leave `activity_binding` unset and preserve older records. Continue using the appropriate entry points for stage artifacts, user confirmation, and principle adoption. A knowledge read alone does not discard progress bound to an earlier revision. After changes to actual inputs or a rerun, verify and record new actual progress. The UI shows the latest record and local time, without inferring live model activity.

## Independent local Web workbench

The Development Kit's `scripts/run_flow.py` starts or reuses the local Web
service after successful `enter/start/select/context`, through the project's
exact `cli_argv`. It preserves the original `task_context` and adds
`workbench_web` with a ready URL or an availability gap. Direct engine
consumers can call `flow web PROJECT`. Preserve project runtime pins.

When `workbench_web.status=ready`, open its exact `url` using Codex
`open_in_codex` with `target.type=browser` and `placement=right`. Open once
on entry/resume and reuse the tab during subsequent work. An open request
does not prove rendering, reading or approval. If that host tool is missing,
return the local URL and retain the gap. Continue authorized engineering work
independently of the browser, an MCP App and renderer acknowledgements.

The loopback service continues independently of the calling tool. The page
polls the existing local version every three seconds while visible and reads
saved stages, document versions, history and check receipts over HTTP. It
does not contact the Server or create a second business state store. Closing
the page or a read failure does not block execution. Stop the service with
`flow web PROJECT --stop`. Stage operations and user decisions remain in the
current conversation under the existing CLI checks.

## Optional MCP delivery diagnostics

The public CLI's `flow enter/start/select` and Workbench-connected `context` register optional delivery diagnostics for the current version. Keep legacy headless runs and library use compatible as `headless_unbound`, with display unverified. Viewing and polling read saved workflows and receipts. Observe changes using the Git change list and file metadata for declared inputs, without scanning or hashing the contents of the entire repository. Detect further edits to already-dirty files, additions, deletions, and declared inputs ignored by Git. Without Git, observe declared files and directories. Mark the result for recheck when observation fails or an old receipt lacks a matching record. The current implementation uses conservative invalidation. Continue to verify actual code targets and evidence before executing or advancing. Stale results cannot satisfy acceptance.

Register `display-delivery.json` for each accepted state update under the exact run. Record the worktree, project, run, stage and attempt, revision, input digests, delivery identity, and outcome. Leave document content and view snapshots out of the registration. Distinguish awaiting display, awaiting render receipt, failed display, and displayed. CLI success, MCP tool return, resource loading, and SDK connection each have their own evidence. A render receipt establishes completed delivery.

Workbench MCP consumes the registered pending work at its own model tool entry points. If the current version lacks a receipt or the previous attempt failed, return the complete Workbench with the current tool response. Do not listen to other tools or make a follow-up display call. Internal polling, ACKs, and exact historical cards do not trigger another card. First delivery uses the complete workflow view and keeps the assess, fix, and verify sidebar navigation for Bug runs. Preserve the original tool `content`, `structuredContent`, and saved results. Leave failed calls recorded as failures. Once the matching version is committed to the DOM and reaches a visible frame, the app-only `formsy_workbench_delivery_ack` sends its exact receipt. A stale version, different run, different delivery, or different owner cannot clear a pending item. When the call times out without a receipt, preserve the failure and allow a later retry. Empty polling after a completed delivery writes no new record.

Generate reviewable configuration using the actual connected service name and path.

```sh
formsy-sop workbench delivery-hooks PROJECT --mcp-server SERVER_NAME --output NEW_HOOKS_JSON
formsy-sop workbench delivery-status PROJECT
```

Use `--input EXISTING_HOOKS_JSON` to merge existing hooks. This command preserves legacy compatibility. Normal display does not generate, install, or depend on a Hook or its trust. Verify the ordinary Workbench tool, card, and ACK sequence in the actual current host. Report a missing or unverified channel. Synthetic host tests provide separate evidence. Missing, late or failed render receipts never reject `advance/check/confirm` or authorized edits. These operations retain their own scope, evidence, approval and pause checks. An ACK records delivery only.

Preserve the independent pause state saved by the old Interrupt integration. Redelivery at an ordinary tool entry point does not clear the pause, call `advance/interact/check/resume`, or resume the model. The host manages its own interruption. Only after the user actually asks to continue, save a request in the current session with `run_id`, `expected_revision`, `operation_id`, `source`, and `message` and run `workbench delivery-resume PROJECT --input REQUEST`. This clears the delivery pause and registers a new display obligation. Development, approval, and resuming the model remain separate. Follow the existing stage authorization and continuation independently of display receipts.

The current version's render ACK confirms that this version loaded at the time recorded. Whether its card is currently expanded remains unknown. CLI-only use remains awaiting display when there is no Workbench request.
